.TH RCTEST 1
.SH NAME
rctest \- regression test for rc's word-list/variable-table foundation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rctest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Rctest
exercises
.IR rc (1)'s
seven translated slices - word lists
.RI ( newword ,
.IR count ,
.IR copywords ,
.IR list2str ,
.IR conclist ,
.IR subwords ),
the variable/keyword tables
.RI ( hash ,
.IR gvlook ,
.IR vlook ,
.IR setvar ,
.IR iskeyword ),
.BR glob.c 's
pure pattern-matching logic
.RI ( deglob ,
.IR match ,
.IR matchfn ,
.IR haswild ),
and
.BR tree.c 's
node constructors
.RI ( tree1 ,
.IR tree2 ,
.IR tree3 ,
.IR mung1 ,
.IR mung2 ,
.IR epimung ,
.IR token ,
plus the real
.B klook
from
.BR var.c ),
and
.BR lex.c 's
tokenizer, from an in-memory string input rather than a real thread's
input stream, and
.BR syn.y 's
own grammar - hand-written recursive descent, exercised here via
.BR treeshape() ,
a test-only structural dump of a parsed tree - and
.BR pcmd.c 's
own
.B %t
tree-to-source pretty-printer, which IS real here
.RI ( treeshape() " is a different, test-only tool for a different"
job), exercised via
.BR testparseprint() " (" parsecmd() " then " pcmd() )
and
.BR testparseprintfn() " (" parsecmd() " then " fnstr() ),
round-tripping this same parser's own real output rather than
hand-building a tree for every case; and
.BR code.c 's
tree-to-instruction-vector compiler
.RI ( outcode() / codeswitch() / iscase() / compile() ),
exercised via
.BR testcompile() " (" parsecmd() " then " compile() " then "
.BR codeshape() ),
the same real-parser-output reuse again
- through
.BR rc.dis 's
own test-seam functions, calling
.B bootstrap()
first to populate the internal tables the same way
.BR rc 's
own
.B init()
would.
.PP
Every pattern that means to use a glob metacharacter spells out the
real escape sentinel explicitly (rune value 2, matching real
.IR rc 's
own
.BR ((char)0x02) ),
embedded as a raw byte written directly into
.BR rc.b 's
own source, not as a textual escape sequence - Limbo parses a literal
control character inside a string literal the same as it would an
escape for the same rune. That byte is invisible in an ordinary
editor: anyone touching a glob pattern here must confirm it survived
whatever tool they used, since silently losing it turns a wildcard
test into a literal-character test with no compile error and no
obviously wrong test name. A bare
.BR * / ? /[
with no escape in front is a literal character to these functions,
not a wildcard, the same as in the real source.
.PP
.B hash() 's
expected values were computed independently in Python from the real
algorithm in
.BR utils/rcsh/var.c
.RI ( h = sum( s [ i ]*( i +1))
.I mod
.IR n ,
renormalized non-negative), not derived from this translation, so a
transcription bug in
.BR rc.b 's
own
.B hash()
would actually be caught.
.PP
.BR conclist() 's
expected result for unequal-length lists - the shorter list's last
word is repeated against the longer list's remaining elements - is
real
.IR rc 's
own documented
.B ^
behavior, traced by hand against the real recursive algorithm in
.B utils/rcsh/word.c
before being written down as a test case, not assumed.
.PP
.BR tree3() 's
one real semantic optimization - a
.B ';'
node with either child nil returns the other child directly instead
of allocating a new node - is checked explicitly (not just the
constructor's ordinary field-setting behavior), since it's the kind of
shortcut a translation can silently lose while still passing every
other test.
.PP
.BR epimung() 's
epilog-chain walk is tested against a chain built directly by hand
(two tokens linked via
.BR mung2 ,
not a real parse), since the chain's shape - not how it was produced -
is all
.B epimung
itself looks at.
.PP
Seven real findings surfaced by tests that initially failed, not by
inspection - see
.IR rc (1)'s
own BUGS for the full traces: a comment's own trailing newline is a
real
.B '\en'
token in real
.IR rc ,
not silently-skipped whitespace;
.B <<[2=1]
/
.B >>[2=1]
genuinely succeed in the real C source because its own HERE/APPEND
dup-rejection check is unreachable dead code there;
.B !
/
.B @
need a following space to be their own token at all, since neither is
in
.BR lex.c 's
own word-stop set;
.B "SWITCH word"
takes a bare word, not a dedicated condition syntax; this parser's own
token-level
.B pskipnl()
exists because the raw, character-level
.B skipnl()
this file's lexer tests already exercise is the wrong thing to call
one layer up, in a grammar action - it would skip characters out from
under a token this recursive-descent parser has already fetched as
lookahead, unlike real yacc's own lazier lookahead timing; and real
.BR pcmd.c 's
own
.B DUP
case both drops the rest of a DUP-prefixed command from its own
display string, and prints its two fd numbers back in the opposite
order from how
.BR lex.c 's
own bracket parser stored them - so a tree parsed from
.B >[2=1]
genuinely prints back as
.BR >[1=2] .
All seven test expectations were corrected to match the real,
verified behavior rather than the translation being changed to match
a wrong assumption.
.PP
The seventh slice's own tests found three real bugs in
.B rc.b
itself, not upstream - see
.IR rc (1)'s
own BUGS for the full traces: the
.B '='
case's epilogue crashed outright on a bare assignment with no
trailing command, because a translation of real code's single
mutable
.B t
(reassigned through the whole function via
.BR c0 / c1 / c2
macros, with an explicit
.B t=tt
reset before returning) dropped that reset, leaving the function's
own
.B t
parameter
.I nil
when the code just past the whole
.B case
block read it; and
.BR codeswitch() " had two bugs of the same shape (real code's own"
mutable-pointer style mistranslated into separate-purpose locals),
producing a structurally wrong instruction sequence for any switch
with more than a trivial body. All three were found by comparing a
test expectation derived from an independent, from-scratch hand-trace
of the real algorithm against what the function under test actually
produced.
